    "unit_id": "OCR_2018_JUN_H432-03_Q01_P_B",
    "prompt": "Table 1.1 shows some properties of Period 3 chlorides. Explain the properties shown in Table 1.1 in terms of bonding and structure.",
    "answer": "NaCl and MgCl₂ have giant ionic lattices with strong electrostatic attractions; ions are fixed in the solid and mobile in the liquid, so the solids conduct poorly and the liquids conduct well. SiCl₄, PCl₃ and SCl₂ are simple molecular substances (simple covalent lattices) with induced dipole–dipole/London forces; these weak intermolecular forces give low melting points, and there are no mobile charge carriers so they conduct poorly in either state. Ionic bonds are stronger than London forces and require more energy to break.",
    "frozen_answer": "NaCl and MgCl₂ have giant ionic lattices with strong electrostatic attractions; ions are fixed in the solid and mobile in the liquid, so the solids conduct poorly and the liquids conduct well. SiCl₄, PCl₃ and SCl₂ are simple molecular substances (simple covalent lattices) with induced dipole–dipole/London forces; these weak intermolecular forces give low melting points, and there are no mobile charge carriers so they conduct poorly in either state. Ionic bonds are stronger than London forces and require more energy to break.",
    "frozen_interpretation": null,
    "mark_scheme": "Award 2 marks for NaCl or MgCl₂: giant ionic/ionic lattice and ions mobile in the liquid state. Award 2 marks for SiCl₄, PCl₃ or SCl₂: simple molecular/simple covalent lattice and induced dipole–dipole/London forces. Award 1 mark for comparing bond strengths: ionic bonds are stronger than London forces or require more energy to break. Ignore aqueous/dissolved ions are mobile, ‘free ions’ and ‘ions are free to carry current’; allow ‘are molecules’, attraction between ions and intermolecular forces. The ionic and molecular labels explain the high/low melting points and conductivity pattern in Table 1.1.",
